<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samuel Kirkland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1741">1741</a>–<a href="/years/1808">1808</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samuel Kirkland was an American Presbyterian missionary to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oneida-people">Oneida</a> and other Iroquois nations. From 1766 he lived among the Oneida, converting many and helping keep them on the American side in the Revolutionary War. In 1793 he founded the Hamilton-Oneida Academy, which became Hamilton College.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samuel-kirkland.jpg" alt="Samuel Kirkland" width="320" height="393" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Samuel_Kirkland_by_Augustus_Rockwell_detail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kirkland was born on 1 December 1741 in Norwich, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>. He was educated in common schools and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princeton-university">College of New Jersey</a>, later Princeton, where he graduated in 1765, and he was soon ordained as a Presbyterian minister. He began his missionary work as a protégé of the Reverend Eleazar Wheelock at Moor's Indian Charity School in Connecticut, which later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-hampshire">New Hampshire</a> as Dartmouth College. There he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-brant">Joseph Brant</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> who became a war leader against the rebels in the Revolutionary War. Kirkland and Wheelock parted company in 1770.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Kirkland worked among the Iroquois of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oneida-people">Oneida</a> and Tuscarora at the western end of the Mohawk River Valley, and studied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iroquoian-languages">Iroquoian languages</a>. He was a long-time friend of the Oneida chief Skenandoa. During the Revolutionary War he acted as adviser and ambassador to the Iroquois, and while four of the Six Nations allied with the British he helped persuade the Oneida and Tuscarora to assist the revolutionaries. The fighting in the Mohawk Valley destroyed frontier settlements and many Iroquois villages alike.</p><p>In 1790 the Italian explorer Paolo Andreani visited him, noting that Kirkland had collected word lists of over 200 languages and held Sunday services with translated psalms. That year Kirkland was elected a Fellow of the American Academy of Arts and Sciences. After the war he helped organise New York State's purchases of land from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oneida-people">Oneida</a> and in the process secured large parcels for himself and his friends; his assistant James Dean was present at every Oneida cession to the state between 1785 and 1818. Some regard him as a peacekeeper between the Iroquois and the settlers, many of them land-hungry migrants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Hamilton-Oneida Academy, which admitted both white and Oneida boys, was named for Treasury Secretary Alexander Hamilton, a member of its first Board of Trustees, and was chartered as Hamilton College in 1812. Kirkland married Jerusha Bingham in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>, on 19 September 1769; of their eleven children, John Thornton Kirkland was President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harvard-university">Harvard College</a> from 1810 to 1828. The town of Kirkland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, and the former women's college Kirkland College, which merged with Hamilton College, bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Samuel Kirkland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Influenced by the Presbyterian missionary <b>Samuel Kirkland</b>, many converted to Christianity in the 18th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/oneida-nation">Oneida Nation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Norwich, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.55,-72.09&amp;year=1784" aria-label="Norwich on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2793 683 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Samuel Kirkland"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3243 953h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3243 953h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.55,-72.09&amp;year=1784">What was here in 1784? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
